{"name":"Bright Loam","hex":"#c1b9aa","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bright","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Emitting much light; visually dazzling; luminous, lucent, radiant.","Of light: brilliant, intense."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Brightness, glow.\n\n Glory, splendour.","Brightness, glow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bright"},{"term":"loam","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of soil; an earthy mixture of sand, silt and clay, with organic matter to which its fertility is chiefly due.","A mixture of sand, clay, and other materials, used in making moulds for large castings, often without a pattern."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover, smear, or fill with loam."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/loam"}],"entries":[{"title":"Loam","blocks":[{"text":"Loam is soil composed mostly of sand, silt, and a smaller amount of clay. By weight, its mineral composition is about 40–40–20% concentration of sand, silt and clay, respectively. These proportions can vary to a degree, however, and result in different types of loam soils: sandy loam, silty loam, clay loam, sandy clay loam, silty clay loam, and loam."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/SoilTexture_USDA.svg/330px-SoilTexture_USDA.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loam","wikidata":"Q1131303"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Lancashire_Loam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_999357.jpg/500px-Lancashire_Loam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_999357.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Lancashire_Loam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_999357.jpg/500px-Lancashire_Loam_-_geograph.org.uk_-_999357.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fine, loam-rich field